Definition:
    -The factory design pattern is basically where an interface or an abstract class is defined for creating the object
     but the object creation is delegated to the subclass of the abstract class or the interface
     So basically instead of using new keyword for creation of objects the class calls methods of the subclasses creating the object
    -So essentially its "stop using the new keyword instead create a dedicated class whose job is to create objects with some logic"


How is it done:
    -We have a "Product" interface and "Concrete Products" classes and then we have a "Creator" interface and the "Concrete Creator"
    -the concrete creator creates object of the type Product
    -In the example we can see a family of Products that is the Notification and then we can see family of Creators that is the Notification Service
    -The Notification service is used to notify and it needs object of the type Notification, but the type of objects created depends on the Concrete creating classes
    -The Notification service has an abstract method of creation of objects which is implemented by its subclasses



Why Factory method:
    -The main use case is of abstracting away the creation of objects